# The guards that keep this repo honest, and the things they guard. `.github/`
# is self-owning, so neither this file nor the CI wiring can change without an
# owner's review. GitHub applies the LAST matching pattern for a given path —
# every pattern here names the same owner, so order is not load-bearing today
# even where two patterns overlap (`tools/oxlint/house/package.json` matches
# both `package.json` and `/tools/oxlint/**`), but a later pattern that overlaps
# an earlier one and names a different owner would win.
/.github/            @chav-aniket
/scripts/            @chav-aniket

# The indirection the guards run through. Owning the workflow without owning
# these leaves the cheapest bypass unreviewed: every CI step calls a guard by
# script name out of `package.json`, `oxlintrc.json` decides that the vendored
# oxlint plugin is the one that runs, and `lefthook.yml` is the local half.
#
# `package.json` and `bunfig.toml` are deliberately unanchored, so they match at
# any depth. A workspace `package.json` can add a lifecycle script or move a
# guard behind a different name, and Bun reads `bunfig.toml` relative to the
# working directory — `deploy.yml` and several package scripts run with `--cwd`,
# so a second one further down the tree would take effect without ever touching
# the root file.
package.json         @chav-aniket
bunfig.toml          @chav-aniket
/oxlintrc.json       @chav-aniket
/lefthook.yml        @chav-aniket

# What the dependency soak actually resolves to. `bunfig.toml` sets the policy;
# these two decide which bytes land. `bun.lock` pins every resolved version and
# integrity hash, and `.bun-version` picks the Bun that installs them and that
# every CI job runs on.
/bun.lock            @chav-aniket
/.bun-version        @chav-aniket

# Production topology. These files name the routes, the D1 and KV bindings and
# the environment each Worker deploys to, so an edit here can point a live
# hostname at a different database without touching a line of application code.
wrangler.toml        @chav-aniket
wrangler.jsonc       @chav-aniket

# Vendored upstream source (see tools/oxlint/anti-slop/README.md). An edit here
# bypasses oxfmt and oxlint by design; the CI guard (verify-vendored-anti-slop.sh)
# catches the shape of a change — `SHA256SUMS` that the tree no longer matches
# its manifest, or a pin in `README.md` left unmoved by a change to the source —
# but it can't say whether the new code is upstream's. That is what a human
# owner is for.
/tools/oxlint/**     @chav-aniket
